        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">AI vs. a live receptionist for a Bentonville office</h2>
        <p>
          Both exist. We will tell you when the live person is the better fit.
          This is a tradeoff, not a ranking.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Live receptionist.</strong> A person on your payroll, or a
          person at a traditional answering service. Strength: a human handles
          anything unexpected, including the emotional call and the caller who
          will not stay inside a script. Weakness: a hired receptionist covers
          the hours you staff. Nights, campus blocks, and the hour you are in
          the car are still uncovered unless you staff them. A traditional
          answering service usually bills per minute or per call, reads a script
          they did not write with you, and hands you a message. The booking is
          still your job the next morning.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>AI receptionist.</strong> Software answers in your voice,
          qualifies by your rules, and books during the call when the rules say
          to book. Strength: flat monthly cost, the same handling at 2 p.m. and
          2 a.m., every call that reaches it recorded, booking finished on the
          call. Weakness: it does not improvise outside the rules you approved.
          Callers with unusual or emotional situations are better served by a
          human, so you keep the escalation path. If you did not write the
          rule, it does not guess one.
        </p>
        <p>
